The '''House of Commons of Sarta''' was the lower house created in 1855 during Sarta's limited liberalisation of the parliamentary system. It was elected by universal suffrage for adult men of the Petran faith and was intended to give the kingdom a wider electoral base without ending the dominance of the lords' chamber.

The house emerged in response to industrial unrest, especially the mining stoppages of 1854 and the growing tension between coastal industrial districts and inland rural communities. By adding an elected chamber, the state hoped to absorb labour grievances into parliamentary debate rather than leave them entirely to street protest.

== Creation ==

The House of Commons was established in 1855 after the lordly parliament approved a reform that created a second chamber alongside the existing upper house of lords. The reform was cautious rather than democratic. It broadened participation, but only within religious and gender limits that excluded much of the population.

The king remained a figurehead of stability, while the lords retained control over domestic affairs, the royal companies, and the appointment of senior officials. The new chamber therefore operated as a balancing body rather than a full popular legislature.

== Franchise ==

Voting rights for the house were extended to adult men of the Petran faith. This gave Petran urban workers, merchants, and landholders a formal role in the state, but it left non-Petrans and all women outside the system. The franchise was universal only within its religious category.

The electoral change was especially significant in the coastal cities, where industrial growth and migration had enlarged the Petran electorate. It also gave the government a way to stage political competition between rival currents without abandoning elite oversight.

== Role ==

The House of Commons became a forum for debate over labour conditions, trade, company policy, and the kingdom's relationship with [[Volisania]]. It did not displace the upper chamber, but it created a new place where coastal reformers and worker-oriented politicians could make their case.

Its creation marked the beginning of a more complicated parliamentary system in Sarta. The state remained oligarchic, but it was no longer governed solely through the lords' chamber.

